      <item>
        <title>AlphaGenome 3: AI-driven whole-genome design — 10Mbp context + variant editing</title>
        <link>https://deepmind.com/alphagenome-3</link>
        <guid isPermaLink="true">https://deepmind.com/alphagenome-3</guid>
        <pubDate>Thu, 28 Jan 2027 00:00:00 GMT</pubDate>
        <source>DeepMind blog</source>
        <description>AlphaGenome 3 is DeepMind&#x27;s 3rd-generation whole-genome model. The v3 release adds generative genome design: given a target phenotype (e.g. &#x27;drought-resistant rice&#x27;), AlphaGenome 3 designs a complete genome (gene sequences + regulatory elements + chromatin structure) that produces the target phenotype with 78% accuracy. Key upgrade: 10Mbp context window (vs v2&#x27;s 1Mbp) — long enough to capture entire gene clusters + topologically-associating domains (TADs) + enhancer-promoter loops in a single prediction. The model is 200B params (MoE 128 experts, top-4 routing). On the GenoDesign-2027 benchmark (50 target phenotypes × 5 species), AlphaGenome 3 achieves 78% phenotype-accuracy (vs v2&#x27;s 61%). 23 of 50 designed genomes have been synthesized + tested in vivo — 18 show the target phenotype.</description>
        <category>Genomics - Genome design</category>
        <dc:creator>DeepMind Genomics</dc:creator>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>NVIDIA Cosmos: 700B-param world foundation model — understands + generates any physical reality</title>
        <link>https://blogs.nvidia.com/cosmos</link>
        <guid isPermaLink="true">https://blogs.nvidia.com/cosmos</guid>
        <pubDate>Sat, 30 Jan 2027 00:00:00 GMT</pubDate>
        <source>NVIDIA blog</source>
        <description>Cosmos is NVIDIA&#x27;s 700B-parameter (MoE 256 experts, top-8 routing) world foundation model. It understands + generates any physical reality: from molecular dynamics to climate, from robotics to game worlds. The model is trained on 10B video clips + 1B physics simulations + 100M 3D meshes. On WorldBench (50 tasks across 5 modalities: video prediction, 3D generation, physics simulation, robotics control, game-world creation), Cosmos achieves 0.87 — the first model to exceed 0.85 on all modalities simultaneously. The key innovation: a unified &#x27;physics-token&#x27; representation that encodes physical laws (conservation, causality, locality) as token embeddings. Released as Cosmos API (cloud) + Cosmos Edge (70B, on RTX 5090). Used in 47 game studios for world generation + 23 robotics companies for sim-to-real + 12 climate institutions for weather visualisation.</description>
        <category>CS - World foundation model</category>
        <dc:creator>NVIDIA Research</dc:creator>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>Claude 5 Max: 10T-param reasoning model with 100M-token thinking — solves Riemann fully</title>
        <link>https://anthropic.com/claude-5-max</link>
        <guid isPermaLink="true">https://anthropic.com/claude-5-max</guid>
        <pubDate>Mon, 01 Feb 2027 00:00:00 GMT</pubDate>
        <source>Anthropic blog</source>
        <description>Claude 5 Max is Anthropic&#x27;s 10-trillion-parameter (MoE 4096 experts, top-32 routing) ultra-reasoning model. The &#x27;Max&#x27; variant extends Claude 5 Ultra&#x27;s thinking budget from 10M to 100M tokens — enabling week-long deductive reasoning. Active params: ~250B per token. On the Riemann Hypothesis Challenge (RH-2027), Claude 5 Max completes the proof that Claude 5 Ultra could not — passing all 10/10 verification checks. The key: the 100M-token budget allows Claude 5 Max to prove the L-function conjecture that was the remaining gap. If peer-reviewed, this would be the first AI-solved Millennium Prize problem. On ARC-AGI-2, Claude 5 Max scores 0.95 — matching the human-expert threshold. On METR (100-step agent tasks), 73%. Released via API; cost: $5000 per 1M thinking tokens (100M × $5000 = $500K per proof attempt).</description>
        <category>CS - Ultra reasoning</category>
        <dc:creator>Anthropic Research</dc:creator>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>ESM-7: 1T-param protein design model — 82% first-try foldable + novel enzyme classes</title>
        <link>https://ai.meta.com/esm-7</link>
        <guid isPermaLink="true">https://ai.meta.com/esm-7</guid>
        <pubDate>Fri, 29 Jan 2027 00:00:00 GMT</pubDate>
        <source>Meta AI blog</source>
        <description>ESM-7 is Meta&#x27;s 1-trillion-parameter (MoE 256 experts, top-8) protein design model. The v7 release pushes the first-try foldable rate to 82% (vs ESM-6&#x27;s 68%, ESM-3&#x27;s 18% in 2024 — 4.5× improvement in 3 years). The curve is approaching saturation. Key innovation: ESM-7 can design novel enzyme classes that don&#x27;t exist in nature. On the EnzymeBench-2027 (50 novel enzyme targets), ESM-7 produces 31/50 with measurable catalytic activity (vs ESM-6&#x27;s 23/50). 7 of these are entirely novel fold architectures not seen in the PDB — the first AI to invent new protein folds. Released under CC-BY-NC 4.0. The 1T-param MoE means only ~80B active per token — deployable on a single NVIDIA B200 (192 GB HBM3e).</description>
        <category>Biology - Protein FM</category>
        <dc:creator>Meta AI FAIR</dc:creator>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title>PsiQuantum Bitternut: 1-million-qubit photonic quantum computer with fault-tolerant operation</title>
        <link>https://psiquantum.com/bitternut</link>
        <guid isPermaLink="true">https://psiquantum.com/bitternut</guid>
        <pubDate>Mon, 25 Jan 2027 00:00:00 GMT</pubDate>
        <source>PsiQuantum blog</source>
        <description>PsiQuantum Bitternut is a 1-million-qubit photonic quantum computer using single-photon sources + linear optical circuits + cryogenic single-photon detectors. The photonic approach avoids the cryogenic dilution refrigerators of superconducting systems — the qubits are photons at room temperature; only the detectors are cryogenic (100mK). Bitternut runs a distance-13 surface code (169 data + 168 ancilla = 337 qubits per logical) achieving 10^-6 logical error rate. The 1M physical qubits support ~2000 logical qubits — sufficient for Shor RSA-4096 (estimated 50M logical-qubit-cycles, ~8 hours). Manufactured by GlobalFoundries on 300mm silicon photonic wafers. Available via PsiQuantum Cloud. The photonic approach enables room-temperature operation + inherent networking (photons can be sent through optical fibre between quantum computers).</description>
        <category>Physics - Photonic quantum computing</category>
        <dc:creator>PsiQuantum + GlobalFoundries</dc:creator>
      </item>
